A||||4||Eric||clutch going?
Z||000000||Eric||06-01-99||03:22 PM||||I noticed some clutch slip today when I shifted into second gear at about 3000 rpm when I got on it.  However, the car seemed to recover quickly.  I only noticed the extra revs for less than one second.  Is this a problem on all V6s or is my clutch just dying.  If it is, how long can I expect it to last.
Z||000001||theogre||06-01-99||05:24 PM||||it's posible you let it out to fast at to high an rpm for the stock clutch, but i'd say it's likely on it's way out. how long it would last only some deity knows.
Z||000002||Eric||06-01-99||06:00 PM||||Ogre, you like the Centerforce clutches, right?  I was wondering if their standard replacement clutches, with an advertised 30% more holding power, would be able to handle the 200+ hp I would get from a 3.2 rebuild.  I don't want to step up to the dual friction because of the cost and the heavier pedal feel.  If their as good as they say they are, then I should be fine, I hope.
Z||000003||theogre||06-01-99||06:36 PM||||I'd ask them. The normal package is designed for stock to "some" HP boost. Not a motor on crack. They can likely put a package together but you need to talk to them.<p>part of the problem in a single disk unit is limmited surface area of the size GM puts on these motors. Even the composite disks that are available are only good to a certain percent of increase over stock. The second problem, which Centerforce adresses to a point, is the constant force diaphram spring. As you've already guessed, increase the spring rate, and increase the pedal load. Centerforce adds a weighting system to the diaphram that tightens the clutch as RPMs rise.
Z||000004||Eric||06-03-99||02:23 PM||||Going, going, gone!  Looks like I'll be starting my engine rebuild a little ahead of schedule.
